Defense wishes to enter a plea of "no contest."

> What happens to the Tournament in this hypothetical case?

The basic idea of #ascend is to make clear the triviality of concern
about end result as opposed to enjoying the process of playing the game.
That's rather antithetical to the fundamental idea of the Tournament.

I suppose you could have a panel of judges watch recorded games and
award points for style. But that doesn't sound like much fun for
anyone involved.

(I should make it clear that I think the Tournament is a good, positive,
useful thing. I thank those who run it and respect those who participate.
#ascend is a rhetorical device, not a serious suggestion. We are
speaking, as David correctly notes, of a hypothetical case.)

> [ Pudding farming and other "cheap" methods of play ]
> Part of the problem, beyond the damage caused to multiplayer setups,

Let's imagine that #ascend also sets your score to the maximum value
that can be represented by whatever integer type is used to store it
on your particular system.

In other words, you cannot get on the high-score list without using
#ascend, and you probably won't even if you do. If you get there, your
reign will be necessarily brief.

I think that characterizing this as "damage" is, at a minimum, overly
broad. It damages that part of the game which involves competing with
other users on the basis of score. That's a tiny, miniscule, nearly
undetectable fraction of the game as a whole.

Now, certainly there are people who enjoy that aspect of the game, and
I have no desire to wreck it just because it holds no interest for me.
But I think there are more fundamental problems with the scoring than
pudding farming and suchlike. Score has almost nothing to do with the
degree of challenge overcome, or degree of success in achieving the
goals set out in the backstory of the game. Score measures nothing
other than itself. Why should I collect dilithium crystals, or haul
around artifact weapons for which I have no use, other than for
points?

> often these abusable techniques are blocking the development of
> interesting gameplay. #ascend is one thing; you either use it or you
> don't.

Adding complexity to the risk/reward equation is generally a good
thing. There may be ways to alter, e.g., pudding farming that would
make for interesting, difficult decisions and encourage thoughtful,
skillful play rather than a mechanical approach.

Ideas like putting a "shot clock" on the midgame do not do this.
They reduce complexity, enormously. One of the most complex and
interesting decisions in the game -- What else can I do to prepare
before I start my run? -- would be reduced to "tick-tock."

Does every single player of 3.4.3 farm puddings? If the answer is no,
why not? Could it be, like #ascend, something you use or you don't?
Yes, it's got a great profit-to-risk ratio. It's also a time-consuming
bore.

> >   3) Maybe we could get past protecting the sacred temple of the
> >      ascended from the threat that a newb might stumble in and wreck
> >      all our hard-earned bragging rights.
> 
> Again, this is a persecution fantasy from you-know-who.

Nethack isn't too easy. There are plenty of people besides we-know-who
who've yet to ascend. For those who have, there are plenty of other
clases, conducts, and combinations of conducts. 

Nethack does have clever, roundabout ways to manipulate the game for
fun and profit. Discovering these, in-game or otherwise, is a big part
of the charm. "Fixing" them requires a delicate hand, not a big
hammer. (I think the poly-abuse "fix" was a little heavy. Stealing
from shops is probably just about right.) If the dev team should err
on the side of caution, good for them.

